00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.629 align:start position:0%
jacques wigley strolls through the  streets of paris  he admires the graffiti on the walls of  his neighborhood  contemporary street artists work in  public spaces as he did four or five  decades ago but in a very different way  he worked with ordinary advertising  posters  laceration is an act of liberation  because the commercial smile wants to  direct you towards a product  politicians want to instruct you  so laceration is a somewhat anarchist  gesture  as a young man biglay would rip ads from  the walls often tearing through several  layers and then frame the results  although people didn't really understand  what he was doing  he was questioned by the police but  never charged  today works by jacques vigley and his  friends raymond ah and francois dufresne  hang in major art museums around the  world  they're the subject of a show at the  sheern gallery in frankfurt documents of  a city's collective consciousness  with all these torn posters jacques  vigley recorded the place and the date  you could say he had a topography of the  city in mind  which is reflected in these posters  another artist who practiced decollage  using fragments of posters ripped off  walls was mimo rotella in rome who  composed works with strips of posters in  a critical reworking of consumerist  motifs  german artist vol fostel worked with  overtly political motifs big lay took a  purely aesthetic approach  i am an observer  i'm interested in what happens  i can take a poster that is leftist or  rightist  without myself tending to the left or to  the right  i wanted to be the painter of my epoch  and the best way of becoming that was to  take the decor of the street  in  a two 1949 a half meters long freeze  for them art was made by all not by one  at his studio in paris biglay has lots  of old material he'd still like to frame  he practiced epheshisma longer than  anyone else for decades in fact  in the 1990s he had to go to cities such  as marseille where such posters could  still be found  in their in other cities they were gone  nowadays with billboards and digital  media poster culture has been  transformed  so what the heirs of afashi's art do is  entirely different  the art of jacques bigley also developed  in the late 1960s he created what he  called a socio-political alphabet  each letter becomes a symbol  he has written entire texts using this  system  these works are now also museum pieces  still he does not really see himself as  a precursor of present-day graffiti art  i did the opposite back then  i took from the street and took it into  the studio  while they work in the street  vigne no longer rips posters off walls  nor does he have to his work is a  successful reflection of four decades of  city life
 

